<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dutch East India Company territories</h1><p class="st-meta">1610 CE – 1799 CE · capital Batavia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Asian empire of the Dutch East India Company, a trading firm that ruled ports and provinces from Batavia and controlled the world spice trade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> (VOC), chartered in 1602, appointed its first Governor-General, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pieter-both">Pieter Both</a>, in 1610. In 1619 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-pieterszoon-coen">Jan Pieterszoon Coen</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jayakarta</a> and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> as its headquarters. The Company then conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-islands">Banda Islands</a> in 1621, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> from the Portuguese in 1641 and forced <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gowa-sultanate">Makassar</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bongaya">Treaty of Bongaya</a> in 1667.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> was governed by the Governor-General and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a>, answering to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heeren-xvii">Heeren XVII</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. The Company enforced monopolies on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, used slave labour and forced deliveries, and intervened in the succession wars of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram Sultanate</a>, gaining much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. Batavia was a multi-ethnic city where Chinese settlers were massacred in 1740.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Corruption, war and falling profits left the Company deeply indebted. It was nationalised by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a> in 1796 and its charter expired on 31 December 1799, when its possessions passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> state and became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dutch-east-india-company-territories.jpg" alt="Dutch East India Company territories" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Himasaram · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Dutch_East_India_Company.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1610 CE – 1799 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Batavia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jan Pieterszoon Coen, Antonio van Diemen, Cornelis Speelman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dutch Reformed Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch, Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>VOC rule, Company rule in the Dutch East Indies, Dutch East India Company</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1704&amp;focus=dutch-east-india-company-territories" aria-label="Dutch East India Company territories on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7639 2407 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dutch East India Company territories lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7959 2663L7839 2352M7959 2663L7941 2659M7959 2663L8005 2686M7959 2663L8005 2686M7959 2663L8052 2725M7959 2663L8152 2721M7959 2663L8152 2696M7959 2663L8134 2697M7959 2663L8065 2716M7959 2663L8053 2725M7959 2663L5097 634M7959 2663L5097 634M7959 2663L5097 634M7